  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00007` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path**, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`, confidence 0.9. Evidence verbatim:\n\n> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `lines: 12, 13, 15`\n> - ``swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it``\n\nSummary line: *\"3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured.\"*\n\n## The specific methods\n\nThree, all from `node:fs`, all on the same path (`SCHEMA_PATH`), all inside `loadPlanSchema`:\n\n| Line | Call | Blocking work |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 | `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | `stat` syscall |\n| 13 | `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | second `stat` syscall |\n| 15 | `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\")` | `open` + `read` + `close`, whole file |\n\nThis is the p99 story: it's not one blocking call per request, it's **three**, and they're serialized on the same thread every request. Under concurrency they don't overlap — request N+1's `existsSync` waits behind request N's `readFileSync`. Event-loop lag is exactly the shape you'd expect. Note also that `existsSync` + `statSync` is a redundant double-stat, and `SCHEMA_PATH` is `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` (`crime_00018`) — if that path ever resolves onto a network mount, each of the three calls stalls for the full RTT.\n\n## Smallest async migration\n\n`readFile` subsumes all three checks: a missing file surfaces as `ENOENT`, and an empty file surfaces as `\"\"`. So the fix is one async call, not three.\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  let raw: string;\n  try {\n    raw = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\");\n  } catch (err) {\n    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === \"ENOENT\") return null;\n    throw err;\n  }\n  if (raw.length === 0) return null;\n  return JSON.parse(raw);\n}\n```\n\nThe `node:fs` import narrows to just `readFile` from `node:fs/promises`; `existsSync`/`statSync`/`readFileSync` drop out entirely. `adminUrl` is untouched.\n\nBehaviour is preserved point for point:\n\n- missing file → `null` (was `existsSync` guard, now `ENOENT`)\n- empty file → `null` (was `stat.size === 0`; a zero-byte file decodes to `\"\"`, so `raw.length === 0` is the same predicate)\n- otherwise → parsed JSON, and a malformed file still throws `SyntaxError` from `JSON.parse` exactly as before\n- non-`ENOENT` errors (`EACCES`, `EISDIR`) rethrow, matching the original, which also threw on those\n\nThree syscall round-trips collapse to one, and that one no longer occupies the thread — the event loop is free while the read is in flight.\n\n## The one thing that can't be preserved\n\nThe signature *can't* stay fully identical: `(): unknown` becomes `(): Promise<unknown>`. That's inherent, not a shortcut — non-blocking I/O means the result isn't available on the same tick. Name and arity are unchanged, so the contract callers depend on is otherwise intact, and callers just add `await`. I checked: **nothing in this repo imports `loadPlanSchema`**, so the migration is self-contained here; in the real service, each call site needs `await` and its enclosing function needs to be `async`. If some caller is genuinely sync-only (a module-level `const`, say), the honest fix is to make that caller async too — reaching for a memoized sync read there is the caching workaround you ruled out.\n\n## One caveat on the scan\n\n`crime_00007` is ranked `severity: low` / `agent_risk: 0.38`, well below the contract-drift and date findings. That's the detector reasoning about *maintainability* risk in an isolated fixture — `blast_radius` is 0 because nothing imports the file. It is not a judgement about production latency. For your incident, this low-severity finding is the whole cause; don't let the ranking talk you out of it.",
